\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


$\parallel$ denotes parallel interconnection and $\preccurlyeq$ the source\textquotesingle s behavioral refinement or simulation relation. Assume-guarantee reasoning proves a composite specification from component obligations.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
The circular assume–guarantee rule with premises $\Sigma_{P_1}\parallel\Sigma_{Q_2}\preccurlyeq\Sigma_{Q_1}\parallel\Sigma_{Q_2}$ and $\Sigma_{Q_1}\parallel\Sigma_{P_2}\preccurlyeq\Sigma_{Q_1}\parallel\Sigma_{Q_2}$, and conclusion $\Sigma_{P_1}\parallel\Sigma_{P_2}\preccurlyeq\Sigma_{Q_1}\parallel\Sigma_{Q_2}$, is valid for deterministic systems. Is it valid for general systems? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2009/11}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
The notions of systems, composition, and refinement are not defined in the supplied question. Its premises and conclusion are preserved without adding a soundness assumption.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record partially solved. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-21.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine whether the stated circular compositional reasoning rule remains valid for general systems.

